{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Adapter Pattern.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Adapter Pattern.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"adapter pattern adapter pattern","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Adapter Pattern.md","fileName":"Adapter Pattern.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Adapter Pattern","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-26 22:56\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Object-Oriented Design Patterns]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Object-Oriented Design Patterns","hands-onobject-orientedprogrammingwithc.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Adapter Pattern.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Adapter Pattern.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"adapter pattern adapter pattern","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Adapter Pattern.md","fileName":"Adapter Pattern.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Adapter Pattern","heading":"Adapter Pattern","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"The Adapter pattern places a translating object between a client and an incompatible interface. The client keeps using the contract it expects, while the adapter converts those calls and values into the form supplied by the existing component.\n\nThis preserves both sides from invasive changes and permits a different adapter for another implementation. The pattern is most useful when the mismatch is stable and translation is clearer than forcing either component to adopt the other's interface.","tags":["baby"],"links":["Object-Oriented Design Patterns","hands-onobject-orientedprogrammingwithc.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Adapter Pattern.md::2","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Adapter Pattern.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"adapter pattern adapter pattern","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Ad